{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PDBRUgTgPYpdYQvXmdP76z","type":"tool_result","content":"48:  packages or uses the network.\n84:   - \"### Network inputs\": every file the change needs from the network, or\n88:     network: true in the \"Worker roles:\" line of the user message, else a\n91:     worker role has network, the status is needs-human.\n92:     When the user message lists network-request.md, read it: it is\n95:     \"### Network inputs\" under \"## Implementation\" even when the status\n134:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. For now the outcome ends like `needs-human`: nothing is pushed, no PR is opened, and the comment lists each entry with its URL in a code span. Part 2 (#385) adds the bounce to the triage engineer, switched off until part 3 lands the lead re-arm (the dependency `closeout.Deps.Network` is left unset in `servecmd`). When on, it applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from closed worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network`; a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human` with the list. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies.\n135-\n136-A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n137-\n138-Close-out checks `plan.md` against the job's base commit, but the committer writes it onto the latest `main`. So at commit time the committer replaces only the job's own `##` section in the current `main` copy (adding it at the end if absent, dropping it if the job removed it) and keeps every other section as `main` has it; a section another role committed after the job's base is not reverted. This runs again on each push retry. If the `main` copy cannot be merged (the job's heading appears twice, or its section holds a heading-like line), the job fails closed as `needs-human` with a `plan.md conflict` reason; like an allowlist denial, only the redacted log and the job's usage record are committed, never `plan.md` or the job's other files.\n139-\n140-The stage in a role file's name is the label stage (`triage`, `worker`, `review`; `config.Kind` and the role directory spell the last one `reviewer`), so a triage and a worker role with the same name never share a file.\n141-\n142-Every `<stage>-<role>.md` starts with frontmatter the controller reads, so the agent reports its outcome without touching GitHub:\n143-\n144-```\n145----\n146-role: engineer\n147-status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human | needs-network (the last three add ard:needs-human for now)\n148-attempt: 1\n149-next: [review:security-review, review:eng-review]   # optional requests\n150-worker: engineer      # lead only: which worker role implements the plan\n151-verdict: approve      # reviewers only: approve | changes (required when status is done)\n152-findings:             # reviewers only, optional: at most 50\n153-  - file: internal/foo/bar.go   # repo-relative slash path (required)\n154-    line: 42                    # 1-based; absent means the whole file\n155-    severity: high              # critical | high | medium | low | info (required)\n156-    blocking: true              # default false\n157-    message: The loop skips the last element.   # required, non-empty\n158-network:            # workers only, with status needs-network: 1 to 10 entries (#384)\n159-  - what: the vendor spec        # one line, at most 200 characters, required\n160-    url: https://example.com/spec.json   # https only, at most 300 bytes, no userinfo, required\n161-    why: The build needs 